    "abstract": "The collapse of the Hudson Bay Ice Saddle (HBIS), whose freshwater signal is dated between 8.6 and 8.5 ka b2k, is increasingly viewed as the primary driver of the abrupt '8.2 ka' cooling anomaly. Yet linking the two implies that the climatic repercussion of the HBIS collapse lagged by centuries \u2013 a delay at odds with some climate models projecting that meltwater forcing can influence climate change within just a few decades. Here, we present a stalagmite-based geochemical record spanning 11.1\u20137.5 ka from western Ireland \u2013 a region highly sensitive to the North Atlantic climate \u2013 that provides direct evidence that the HBIS collapse triggered a rapid hydroclimatic change in the region. Based on the stalagmite \u03b418O, mirroring North Atlantic seawater \u03b418O, and its Sr/Ca data, we document a pronounced increase in precipitation coinciding with the HBIS collapse and a slowdown of the AMOC at 8.6 ka. This precipitation trend persists throughout the '8.2 ka' event and does not return to the level pre-HBIS collapse until 7.9 ka, suggesting a slow recovery of the hydroclimate, independent of temperature direction. Our findings outline what may lie ahead for NW Europe in the coming decades if meltwater flux keeps accelerating and the AMOC weakens.",
    "field": "Paleoclimatology",
    "problem": "The climatic impact of the Hudson Bay Ice Saddle collapse is debated due to an apparent centuries-long lag before the '8.2 ka' cooling event, a delay that contradicts climate models projecting rapid climatic responses to meltwater forcing within just a few decades.",
    "method": "We present a stalagmite-based geochemical record (\u03b4\u00b9\u2078O and Sr/Ca) from western Ireland to provide direct evidence that the Hudson Bay Ice Saddle collapse triggered a rapid and sustained increase in precipitation in NW Europe, resolving the apparent time lag with the '8.2 ka' cooling event.",
    "task": "The task is to use a stalagmite-based geochemical record from western Ireland to demonstrate that the collapse of the Hudson Bay Ice Saddle triggered a rapid and pronounced increase in precipitation in Northwest Europe approximately 8.6 ka ago.",
    "model": [
        "ERA5",
        "EC-Earth3",
        "BISICLES"
    ],
